# tools/al/ales_glide_witness.rish — the gliding delay: a fractional delay read by linear interpolation (ALES70).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES70.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260814/20260814-194031_fill-ales70-lotus-gliding-interpolated-delay.md
# Stands on: lotus/taps.rye (ALES68 — the multi-tap whose fixed integer taps this generalizes to a moving fractional one, and whose
# snapshot-before-write idiom this reuses); lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 — the Clip, sample_max, max_clip, and the one true saturate).
#
# lotus/glide.rye introduces the genuinely new arithmetic the whole modulated-delay family needs: a delay read at a FRACTIONAL position by
# LINEAR INTERPOLATION of its two neighbours. The delay is carried in fixed point (scaled by glide_scale = 256) and glides linearly from
# d0_scaled to d1_scaled across the span; each read is delayed = (near·(scale-frac) + far·frac)/scale off a frozen dry snapshot, mixed dry
# at num/den and saturated once. The selftest proves: level = 0 is the dry identity; a whole-sample glide is an EXACT integer tap (equal to
# ALES68's one-tap multi-tap byte-for-byte — interpolation exact at integer positions); the half-sample is the EXACT average of two
# neighbours (a 2.5-sample delay, computed by hand); the glide honors its ramp endpoints (d0 at the first span sample, d1 at the last) and
# moves monotonically, a falling glide too; a loud wet+dry sum saturates rather than wraps; a sub-sample or too-wide delay refuses
# BadDelay, a wet above unity or a zero denominator BadLevel, an out-of-range span BadRange. Purely local — no lookahead beyond the
# snapshot read, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no sample rate.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/al/ales_glide_witness.rish

say "ales-glide: Language EN — every delay so far reads the line at a WHOLE-sample position (the feedback echo, the multi-tap). The chorus, the flanger, and the vibrato all share one move those cannot make: the delay MOVES, gliding through positions BETWEEN two samples. A delay of two-and-a-half samples has no sample sitting there; the honest read is the LINEAR INTERPOLATION of the two that straddle it. This rung introduces that interpolation — the one genuinely new arithmetic the modulated-delay family needs — in its simplest honest form, a delay that glides linearly from a start to an end position across the span (the flanger's sweep)."
say "ales-glide: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively: a fractional delay IS the linear interpolation of its two neighbours. The delay is carried in fixed point and the read is delayed = (near·(scale-frac) + far·frac)/scale off a FROZEN dry snapshot (the ALES68 idiom, reused), so a moving read never reads a sample it just wrote; at frac = 0 the read is the nearer sample exactly (a whole-sample glide is an exact integer tap), and at the half-sample it is the exact average of the two neighbours."
say "ales-glide: Lens TAME — one clip of i16 PCM, a u32 span, the scaled delays d0/d1 and a plain u32/u32 wet level; the dry prefix is snapshotted into one bounded [max_clip]i16 scratch (taps' and graft's width), the ramp, interpolation, and wet mix all run in i64 and saturate once, so nothing on the path can overflow; a scaled delay under one whole sample or a whole delay past max_clip refuses BadDelay, a wet above unity or a zero denominator BadLevel, a span outside the samples BadRange, all before any write, the clip untouched on refusal; siloed to lotus/, the delay in scaled sample indices not milliseconds, no lookahead, no network, no keys, no funds, no device."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/glide"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/glide.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-glide: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/glide.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-glide: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-glide: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-glide' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-glide: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-glide line"

# This rung generalizes ALES68's fixed integer tap to a moving fractional one and proves equality at integer positions over its public API —
# re-prove the multi-tap still stands green beside it.
let taps = run ["sh" "-c" "env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/taps.rye -femit-bin=lotus/bin/taps && lotus/bin/taps selftest 2>&1 | grep -q 'GREEN ales-taps' && echo yes"]
assert (trim taps.out) == "yes" else "ales-glide: the ALES68 multi-tap delay regressed under the gliding delay"

say "ales-glide: GREEN — the gliding delay reads a fractional delay by linear interpolation, the genuinely new arithmetic the whole modulated-delay family needs: delayed = (near·(scale-frac) + far·frac)/scale off a frozen dry snapshot, mixed dry and saturated once. level = 0 is the dry identity; a whole-sample glide is an EXACT integer tap (equal to ALES68's one-tap multi-tap byte-for-byte — interpolation exact at integer positions); the half-sample is the EXACT average of two neighbours (a 2.5-sample delay, computed by hand); the glide honors its ramp endpoints and moves monotonically, a falling glide too; a loud wet+dry sum saturates rather than wraps; a sub-sample or too-wide delay refuses BadDelay, a wet above unity or a zero denominator BadLevel, an out-of-range span BadRange, each leaving the clip untouched; the ALES68 multi-tap stays GREEN beside it. The periodic modulated delay (chorus, flanger, vibrato) — a triangle or sine LFO driving the delay round a centre — is a thin later rung that only chooses how the scaled delay moves."
